I can’t read the repository from this chat, but the stack trace plus the reported scenario point to a very specific root cause.

## Root cause

In `src/chess.ts`, the pawn move generation computes empty squares as:

```ts
const empty = ~occupied;
```

In JavaScript, `BigInt` bitwise NOT produces an infinite-precision negative BigInt with all higher bits set. A white pawn on the 8th rank, e.g. `h8` (`1n << 63n`), shifted left by `8n` produces a bit above the 64th square. Because `empty` also has that high bit set, the move generator emits a destination square index ≥ 64. That later becomes an undefined square name, and `_movePiece` XORs the zobrist key with `undefined`, causing:

```
TypeError: Cannot mix BigInt and other types, use explicit conversions
```

The fix is to mask `empty` to the 64-bit board.

## Code change

In `src/chess.ts`, change:

```ts
const empty = ~occupied;
```

to:

```ts
const empty = ~occupied & MASK;
```

Make sure `MASK` is imported from `./bitboard` (it already exists there as `(1n << 64n) - 1n`). If it is not already imported, add it to the existing import:

```ts
import { MASK } from './bitboard';
```

## Regression test

Create a new file `test/regression-pawn-edge.test.ts`:

```ts
import { Chess } from '../src/chess';
import { describe, expect, it } from 'vitest';

describe('Regression: pawn on edge rank', () => {
  it('does not throw when generating verbose moves for a white pawn on h8', () => {
    const chess = new Chess();
    chess.put({ type: 'p', color: 'w' }, 'h8');

    expect(() => chess.moves({ square: 'h8', verbose: true })).not.toThrow();
    expect(chess.moves({ square: 'h8', verbose: true })).toEqual([]);
  });
});
```

## Unified diff (best-effort)

If the line numbers in your checkout match, this should apply:

```diff
diff --git a/src/chess.ts b/src/chess.ts
--- a/src/chess.ts
+++ b/src/chess.ts
@@ -1489,7 +1489,7 @@ export class Chess {
     const from = square ?? this._board.findIndex((p) => p?.color === us);
     const piece = this._board[from];
     if (!piece) return [];

     const occupied = this._board.reduce((acc, p, i) => (p ? acc | (1n << BigInt(i)) : acc), 0n);
-    const empty = ~occupied;
+    const empty = ~occupied & MASK;
     const enemies = this._board.reduce((acc, p, i) => (p && p.color === opponents ? acc | (1n << BigInt(i)) : acc), 0n);
```

If that exact hunk does not apply, the only real change is the one-liner above.

